type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for fair test. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about fair test, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Changing length and pluck force together confounds the test.. Correction: Same ruler type/pluck force; with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y4-w14-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise fair test.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Physical World","success_criteria":["I can use fair test with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify pitch and explain why it matters.","I can practise variable independently on a new prompt.","I can notice length needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A fair test of pitch changes one variable (e.g.","options":["A fair test of pitch changes one variable (e.g","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair test with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBottles with different water levels: blow or tap the same way. Higher air column often → different pitch. Record water height vs pitch order.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan a fair pitch test with rulers hanging over a desk edge at different lengths. What stays the same?\n\nFEEDBACK\nSame ruler type/pluck force; only overhang length changes; same listener distance.","explain":"A fair test of pitch changes one variable (e.g. string length or water level in bottles) while keeping striking method and other factors the same.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing length and pluck force together confounds the test. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Pitch fair test.","caption":"Do not change two things.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">One variable</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">length / water</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Same strike</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">fair test</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Bottles with different water levels: blow or tap the same way. Higher air column often → different pitch. Record water h Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Bottles with different water levels: blow or tap the same way. Higher air column often → different pitch. Record water h Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair test with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBottles with different water levels: blow or tap the same way. Higher air column often → different pitch. Record water height vs pitch order. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Fair length test.","caption":"Full write-up.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Results table</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">pattern</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">confounded pluck</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about fair test with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nChanging length and pluck force together confounds the test. Here is a more productive model: Bottles with different water levels: blow or tap the same way. Higher air column often → different pitch. Record water height vs pitch order.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSame ruler type/pluck force; only overhang length changes; same listener distance.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evi